WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:13.240
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: OK then the next paper is Jane's,  it's back to you, but I hope also with Victor

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.940
on strengthening public participation in NHS England

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:28.300
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: My view is that we have
made good progress on designing some policy

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.590
guidance for NHS England, which, in my view,
goes beyond the minimum required in Section

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:39.910
13Q of the Health and Social Care Bill. There
is some good practice in engaging the public.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:46.160
I am pleased to see that we have not abandoned
NHS Citizens completely; version two is being

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:51.560
rolled out, or is being looked at, anyway.
The task force that you asked me to chair,

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.160
Malcolm, is coming to the end of its first
phase, and I am hoping that one of the products

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:00.220
of that first phase will be a dashboard which
will allow NHS England as a board to get line

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:08.410
of sight of what is happening out there, and
how it contributes to our core business commissioning.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:14.039
We are consulting on the next phase of the
task force. There is still work to do, in

00:01:14.039 --> 00:01:19.630
my view, in corralling and understanding the
role of the voluntary community sector in

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:22.929
the delivery of health - and social care,
actually - in the business; I think there

00:01:22.929 --> 00:01:27.090
is a lot of work to do there.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:34.759
There is a notion, which I have tried to dismiss,
really, that public-patient engagement is

00:01:34.759 --> 00:01:42.490
somehow the same as comms and public relations,
and it is not. This is something which we

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:48.889
need to embed in the way we do business as
usual, and we are still a way from doing that.

00:01:48.889 --> 00:01:53.950
I am hopeful that what will come out of tomorrow's
announcement will assist in that regard, but

00:01:53.950 --> 00:02:00.560
that is all I have to say on the report, and
I am sure Jane might want to add some detail.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:12.800
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Thank you, Victor. Building
on that, the paper just basically gives you

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:17.870
what we promised to do. Back in 2015, you
approved the last policy. We said we would

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:24.590
come back with an update on progress and a
revised version, and that is what this does.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:29.060
We have deliberately attempted to go, or are
going, much further than the minimum statutory

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:34.280
13Q requirements, and there are a multitude
of ways in which you can truly engage with

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.291
patients and the public. The important thing
for us is to make sure that we do that in

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:44.060
a meaningful way and actually listen to people
in the system, and base it locally rather

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:49.340
than necessarily trying to do it top-down
from NHS England. We have some great support

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:54.239
from people in enabling us to do that; including,
I have to say, from a gentleman who is sitting

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:56.549
in our audience today.

00:02:56.549 --> 00:03:03.409
We have in here an update on our ten-point
plan, which does show that, overall, we have

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:07.500
made some significant progress. A lot of that
has been around training, particularly of

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.019
our staff, but also of patient and public
voice partners, which is really important.

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:17.469
We have also established the involvement hub,
which actually gives people an opportunity

00:03:17.469 --> 00:03:21.189
to access some of that information. There
is a good that the paper highlights, that

00:03:21.189 --> 00:03:26.060
within the first three months we had something
like 30,000 views of that, so really having

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.060
an impact.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:33.550
One of the things we have tried to do is embed
patient and public voice into our priority

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:41.340
programmes. As an example, we have produced
specific guidance around our different commissioning

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:48.990
areas, so that the teams that are looking
after, for example, health and justice, specialised

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:54.469
commissioning, have dedicated and specific
support to do the patient and public voice

00:03:54.469 --> 00:03:58.769
engagement in those areas, which has been
good. We set up an advice and support service

00:03:58.769 --> 00:04:04.569
so that people could access expert advice
if they needed it, and we get about 30 requests

00:04:04.569 --> 00:04:11.999
a month for that, which is helpful, so people
can provide that level of support.

00:04:11.999 --> 00:04:17.120
We are in the process, as I think Victor mentioned
briefly, of developing a dashboard, so we

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:23.500
can try and find some sensible ways of updating
and providing some more information for the

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.700
Board on what we are doing and how we are
doing it, so making sure it is meaningful,

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:34.270
not tick-box, and it is actually linked to
what our priority areas are, but also not

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:40.430
forgetting that other people will have a valuable
and important role to play in actually giving

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:43.230
us feedback.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:51.380
Then the revised policy, which is attached
for your approval. We said that we would come

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:58.530
back by the end of March 2017 with a review
of that policy. We have invited partner organisations

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:04.170
to give us their views; we have had about
106 different responses. The sorts of messages

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:08.790
we had were, 'Please can you connect it better,'
'Can you reduce it in length,' 'Can you make

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:15.420
it simpler,' and so we have responded to that
and we have turned two documents into one,

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.980
we have made it more straightforward and we
have tried to make it more relevant to our

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:25.150
particular commissioning arrangements. It
feels like it is in an easier place; it is

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:32.280
a simpler read than the previous two. I think
we now need to focus on how we implement and

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:37.130
support the STPs, and we are looking at ways
of doing that at the moment with the central

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:43.490
team providing direct support to STPs, so
that the PPV engagement in STPs is the best

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:48.800
it possibly can be. That is a really important
priority for the programme moving forwards,

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:56.620
and I think we have the mechanisms and the
expertise in place to be able to do that now.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:03.480
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: This is obviously a really
important area, so I have a couple of questions.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:11.040
One was on the 106 responses to the new policy.
In the context of the 800 patient and public

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:17.470
voice partners, did that feel like a good
number of responses?

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:25.311
The second thing was thinking about what our
investment and resource is in this area. As

00:06:25.311 --> 00:06:35.600
we think about helping STPs with their consultation
requirements, how much do we spend on patient

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.750
and public involvement here? Can we get a
sense of, for example, how many people we

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:45.210
have whose primary job is around this in our
own organisation?

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:51.290
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: I cannot give you it off
the top of my head; I would have to go back

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:56.680
to my team and check. We have the patient
and public involvement division of my directorate,

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:02.880
and I also have a patient experience team.
Within the PPV team, there are people dedicated

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.900
to this part of the work. We also have people
who look at insight and surveys, who also

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:12.200
hold things like focus groups and report on
things like friends and family tests, national

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:17.060
surveys. We also have a patient-centred care
team that does quite a bit of work on that,

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:20.600
and we have access out to different voluntary
groups and voluntary organisations that come

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:23.820
together to provide us with support. Overall,
there is quite a lot of support.

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:31.550
The other thing: as well as the 106 responses,
we also held quite a few meetings, training

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:37.670
sessions, focus groups, so the individuals
involved in those have not been listed separately,

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:41.960
but they also contributed to the feedback.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:46.120
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: In terms of quantifying the
resource, that will take a bit of -

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:48.100
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: It will be relatively straightforward
to do; I just cannot give it to you off the

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.100
top of my head.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:55.040
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: It is probably also worth
noting that we do work in strong partnership

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:58.140
with Healthwatch, so it is not as though we
are the only game in town.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.140
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Victor, can I just say thank
you again for taking on the chairmanship of

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:00.300
the task and finish group, and for the way
in which that has fed into this report, for

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:06.320
the revision of the public policy which we
will apply across the organisation? Also,

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:11.090
it is an area in which the Board will continue
to take a strong interest and wish to oversee

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:12.610
developments as they occur.

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:18.930
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I would also like to thank
Michelle, she has played a blinder on this.

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:22.040
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Yes, thank you indeed, Michelle.

